  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["D2", "R3", "J5", "U6", "U1", "J6"],
      "pins": ["U6.1", "U6.2", "U6.7", "U1.13", "J5.2", "J6.2"],
      "nets": ["HEATER"],
      "observed": "On the Microcontroller sheet (mcu.kicad_sch, tile 1/4) the global net label 'HEATER' is attached to U1 pin 13 (GPIO1), to U6 pins 1 and 2 (DIAG_EN and IN), and to J5 pin 2 and the D2/R3 indicator branch. The actual switched heater output, U6 pin 7 (OUT), is wired only to J5's sibling connector J6 pin 2 and carries no 'HEATER' label. So the status LED D2 + R3 and an extra screw terminal J5 sit on the GPIO1 control net, while the power-switched output net is unlabeled.",
      "expected": "The specification states that the global 'HEATER' net is the switched output: 'OUT (pin 7) feeds the heater connector J6 pin 2, ... A status LED (D2) and series resistor (R3) are also on the HEATER net, so the heater output doubles as a visual heater on indicator', while the drive signal is the hierarchical net '/Microcontroller/HEATER' on 'IN/DIAG_EN (pins 1/2) ... driven from ESP32 GPIO1'. D2/R3 must therefore be on the U6 OUT net, and there must be no screw terminal (J5) tying the GPIO1 control net to GND.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "OUT (pin 7) feeds the heater connector J6 pin 2 ... A status LED (D2) and series resistor (R3) are also on the HEATER net, so the heater output doubles as a visual 'heater on' indicator"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "IN/DIAG_EN (pins 1/2) are both driven from ESP32 GPIO1 (net /Microcontroller/HEATER)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "mcu.kicad_sch: green global label HEATER joins U1.13 (GPIO1), U6.1 (DIAG_EN), U6.2 (IN), J5.2 and D2/R3; U6.7 (OUT) connects to J6.2 with no HEATER label"}
      ],
      "impact": "The 'heater on' LED now follows the 3.3 V GPIO1 command instead of the 12 V switched output, so it can light even when U6 is open, the element is disconnected, or the output is shorted, giving a false heater-on indication; and the extra terminal J5 provides a user-accessible path that shorts the MCU control pin to GND when mated.",
      "confidence": 0.7
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The build deviates on the heater path: the global net 'HEATER' is wired to the GPIO1 control side (U6 IN/DIAG_EN) together with the status LED D2/R3 and an undocumented extra screw terminal J5, leaving the true switched output U6 OUT -> J6 unlabeled, whereas the specification places D2/R3 on the heater output net and the control signal on a separate hierarchical net."
}

